<section id="harness" class="section" aria-labelledby="harness-title">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2 id="harness-title">07 Harness with Policy</h2>
    <section
      id="harness-setting"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="harness-setting-title"
    >
      <h3 id="harness-setting-title">Evaluation Setting</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          The comparisons above suggest complementary strengths: GPT-6-Astra interprets tasks and
          recovers disrupted goals, whereas post-trained policies execute precise and bimanual
          contact more reliably. The harness with policy combines the two. Following{' '}
          <a
            class="source-link"
            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08448"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            Harness VLA
          </a>{' '}
          <a
            class="paper-citation"
            href="#ref-harness-vla"
            role="doc-biblioref"
            aria-label="Reference 2"
          >
            [2]
          </a>
          , implemented in RPent{' '}
          <a class="paper-citation" href="#ref-rpent" role="doc-biblioref" aria-label="Reference 3">
            [3]
          </a>
          , it exposes a frozen post-trained policy as a callable tool alongside the EEF waypoint
          interface, together with a call that returns the annotated demonstration on request. In
          each invocation, GPT-6-Astra assigns the policy 1–300 physics steps. Within these steps,
          the policy acts without intervention from the agent and then returns new visual and
          proprioceptive observations.
        </p>
        <p>
          We evaluate two policies post-trained on EBench demonstrations across all 510 test
          episodes: the VLA π₀.₅, which acts on the current observation and replans every 30 physics
          steps, and the WAM InternW0-Δ, which also conditions on two earlier frames and replans
          every 10 physics steps. Evaluated alone, π₀.₅ trails GPT-6-Astra on both metrics, and
          InternW0-Δ exceeds it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section
      id="harness-results"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="harness-results-title"
    >
      <h3 id="harness-results-title">Overall Performance</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          Each harness configuration outperforms both of its components. Overall SR rises from 46.7%
          under direct control to <strong>53.0% with π₀.₅ and 56.0% with InternW0-Δ</strong>, and
          the Score from 0.654 to 0.692 and 0.744. With InternW0-Δ, the harness surpasses OpenWAM-α,
          the strongest post-trained policy, on both metrics. The gains concentrate on long-horizon
          and tabletop tasks, where the policies alone outperform direct control, whereas on
          high-precision tasks both configurations remain below the policies alone. Under
          distribution shifts, the harness with InternW0-Δ improves on all four conditions and
          reaches 53.1% under mixed perturbations, while the harness with π₀.₅ gains mainly on new
          objects and instructions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div id="harness-table"></div>
    </section>
    <section id="harness-tasks" class="case-study-section" aria-labelledby="harness-tasks-title">
      <h3 id="harness-tasks-title">Where the Gains Land</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          Across the 26 tasks, the gain of the harness over direct control follows the advantage of
          the policy alone. The largest gains arise on Glass into glassbox and Dishwasher, where the
          policy executes the bimanual transport, lid closure, and rack manipulation that direct
          control does not finish. On Peg in hole, Pen, Microwave, and Teacup &amp; teapot, where
          neither policy alone exceeds direct control, both configurations fall below it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="harness-figure" data-harness-figure="tasks"></div>
    </section>
    <section
      id="case-harness-gain"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="case-harness-gain-title"
    >
      <h3 id="case-harness-gain-title">Where the Policy Helps</h3>
      <div class="case-study-content"></div>
    </section>
    <section
      id="harness-episodes"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="harness-episodes-title"
    >
      <h3 id="harness-episodes-title">Episodes Won and Lost</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          The net gains combine turnover in both directions over the 510 paired episodes. Most newly
          successful episodes exhausted the physics-step limit under direct control, and the policy
          completes their remaining stages. The newly failed episodes end differently with the two
          policies. With π₀.₅, half of them begin when the agent's own grasp slips. With InternW0-Δ,
          most lose an object during a policy segment or just after the agent resumes control.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="harness-figure" data-harness-figure="episodes"></div>
    </section>
    <section
      id="harness-delegation"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="harness-delegation-title"
    >
      <h3 id="harness-delegation-title">Who Drives the Robot</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          Every episode starts with a policy invocation, and the policy executes nearly all physics
          steps in the first tenth. GPT-6-Astra then steps in with grasp tests, corrections, and
          recoveries. Successful episodes hand the final stages back to the policy, whereas
          unsuccessful ones end largely under the agent's own commands. The delegation follows task
          horizon rather than the competence of the policy: on the seven tasks where π₀.₅ alone
          succeeds in at most 10% of episodes, the agent still leaves it 60% of the physics steps.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="harness-figure" data-harness-figure="delegation"></div>
    </section>
    <section
      id="case-harness-loss"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="case-harness-loss-title"
    >
      <h3 id="case-harness-loss-title">Where the Coordination Breaks</h3>
      <div class="case-study-content"></div>
    </section>
    <section id="harness-cost" class="case-study-section" aria-labelledby="harness-cost-title">
      <h3 id="harness-cost-title">Token Cost</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          Invoking a policy also moves computation from the agent into the policy. GPT-6-Astra makes
          about half as many tool calls per episode, and its tokens per success fall by more than
          half. The savings concentrate on long-horizon tasks, where one success costs less than two
          fifths of its tokens under direct control. In exchange, the policy runs 45 inferences of
          π₀.₅ or 135 of InternW0-Δ per episode.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="harness-figure" data-harness-figure="tokens"></div>
      <div class="case-insight">
        <p>
          With a post-trained policy, GPT-6-Astra reaches further on long-horizon and tabletop tasks
          at a lower token cost per success. It remains bounded by high-precision contact, which
          none of its components masters, and by loose coordination: the agent delegates by horizon
          rather than by the competence of the policy, the policy receives the task instruction
          instead of the agent's subgoal, and grasps left by a policy segment go unmaintained.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>
